    {
      "title": "Advanced idioms, proverbs and figures of speech",
      "html": "<p>Senior candidates must recognise <b>advanced idioms</b> \u2014 fixed expressions whose meaning is not literal.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><i>a wolf in sheep's clothing</i> \u2014 a dangerous person pretending to be harmless.</li>\n    <li><i>bite the bullet</i> \u2014 accept an unpleasant duty bravely.</li>\n    <li><i>call it a day</i> \u2014 stop working.</li>\n    <li><i>cut corners</i> \u2014 do something in the easiest but poorest way.</li>\n    <li><i>the ball is in your court</i> \u2014 it is your turn to decide.</li>\n    <li><i>throw in the towel</i> \u2014 give up.</li>\n    <li><i>hit the nail on the head</i> \u2014 describe something exactly right.</li>\n    <li><i>a storm in a teacup</i> \u2014 a big fuss about a small matter.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>Proverbs</b> compress wisdom: <i>A stitch in time saves nine; too many cooks spoil the broth; every cloud has a silver lining; when the going gets tough, the tough get going.</i></p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Explain <i>throw in the towel</i>.",
          "a": "To give up (from boxing, where the trainer throws in a towel to signal surrender)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Registers \u2014 Aviation, Maritime, Commerce, Banking, Administration",
      "html": "<p><b>Aviation</b>: cockpit, cabin crew, altitude, turbulence, runway, taxiing, boarding pass, aviation fuel, air-traffic control, hangar, jet lag, itinerary, callsign.</p>\n  <p><b>Maritime</b>: vessel, cargo, port, harbour, quay, buoy, anchor, stevedore, freight, bill of lading, keel, deck, mast, tanker.</p>\n  <p><b>Commerce</b>: retailer, wholesaler, invoice, quotation, discount, dispatch, warehouse, consignment, gross, net, turnover, credit facility.</p>\n  <p><b>Banking</b>: deposit, withdrawal, ledger, overdraft, credit balance, interest rate, mortgage, cheque, standing order, remittance, teller.</p>\n  <p><b>Administration</b>: memorandum, agenda, minutes, quorum, resolution, ex-officio, delegation, dossier, protocol, secretariat.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Aviation.</li><li>Banking term.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Register: <i>cockpit</i> belongs to:<br/><b>A:</b> Aviation</p><p><b>Q:</b> Register: <i>overdraft</i>:<br/><b>A:</b> Banking</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which register is <i>bill of lading</i>?",
          "a": "Maritime (shipping) \u2014 the document listing goods being shipped."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Advanced word study",
      "html": "<p>Beyond memorising lists, examine words by their <b>parts</b>:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Prefix</b> \u2014 added at the front (<i>un-, dis-, mis-, over-, anti-, pre-</i>): <i>uneducated, mislead, overrule, anti-social.</i></li>\n    <li><b>Root</b> \u2014 the core meaning (<i>bene</i> = good; <i>ject</i> = throw; <i>graph</i> = write).</li>\n    <li><b>Suffix</b> \u2014 added at the end (<i>-ness, -ity, -ment, -tion, -able</i>): <i>happiness, ability, movement, decision, readable.</i></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Use word roots to guess meaning: <i>benefactor</i> \u2014 someone who <b>does good</b> (bene + factor).</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Against.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Word from parts: <i>anti- + social</i> means:<br/><b>A:</b> opposed to society</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Word from parts: <i>anti- + social</i> means: \u2014 opposed to society.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Break down <i>unhappiness</i>: prefix / root / suffix.",
          "a": "un- (prefix, not) + happy (root) + -ness (suffix, state). Meaning: state of not being happy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Word formation \u2014 prefixes, roots, suffixes",
      "html": "<p><b>Word formation \u2014 prefixes, roots, suffixes</b> is a key part of Vocabulary Development (SSS 3, English). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Beyond memorising lists, examine words by their parts : Prefix \u2014 added at the front ( un-, dis-, mis-, over-, anti-, pre- ): uneducated, mislead, overrule, anti-social.</li><li>Use word roots to guess meaning: benefactor \u2014 someone who does good (bene + factor).</li><li>Worked example Q: Word from parts: anti- + social means: A: opposed to society</li><li>To give wrong information.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Word from parts: <i>anti- + social</i> means:<br/><b>A:</b> opposed to society \u2014 Against.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Word from parts: <i>anti- + social</i> means:",
          "a": "opposed to society \u2014 Against."
        }
      ]
